# config/subsys/Kconfig.display — Display, framebuffer, input server and compositor

config DRV_DISPLAY_HDMI
    # cargo-feature: hdmi
    bool "HDMI framebuffer (VF2 DC8200 + HDMI TX)"
    default n
    depends on BOARD_VF2
    help
      Kernel feature `hdmi` (crates/drivers/display). Fixed resolution
      (HDMI_WIDTH/HDMI_HEIGHT, "Platform / Board"), no EDID, solid-colour
      output only. Never validated on hardware: QEMU has no model of this
      peripheral, and the PHY calibration is placeholder data, so expect
      no signal on a real monitor. The gate builds it ("hdmi" row); no
      row boots it.

config DRV_DISPLAY_RAMFB
    # cargo-feature: ramfb
    bool "QEMU ramfb framebuffer"
    default n
    depends on BOARD_QEMU && ARCH_RISCV64
    imply FB_GENERIC
    help
      Kernel feature `ramfb` (crates/drivers/display/src/ramfb.rs): a
      framebuffer source for FB_GENERIC. Allocates the pixel buffer,
      configures QEMU's ramfb device through fw_cfg and hands the geometry
      to the generic framebuffer. Needs `-device ramfb` on the QEMU command
      line (and a display backend to see it). Not the VF2 display driver.
      riscv64 only: the fw_cfg address it uses is defined for the riscv64
      QEMU virt map alone (an aarch64 build does not compile).

config FB_GENERIC
    # cargo-feature: fb-generic
    bool "Generic framebuffer (virtio-gpu, ramfb, Limine, device-tree simple-framebuffer)"
    default n
    help
      Kernel feature `fb-generic` (crates/drivers/fb, kernel/src/boot/
      fb.rs): one framebuffer object (geometry, stride, pixel format) fed
      by whichever source the machine has: a virtio-gpu device (QEMU
      `-device virtio-gpu-device` on riscv64/aarch64 virt and x86_64
      microvm, `-device virtio-gpu-pci` with DRV_PCI; the mode and refresh
      rate come from its EDID; the pixels are RAM the driver allocates and
      every drawn rectangle is committed to the display through the flip
      hook), QEMU ramfb (DRV_DISPLAY_RAMFB), the framebuffer the Limine
      loader set up (x86_64), or a device-tree `simple-framebuffer` node
      (riscv64, aarch64). The others are mapped
      write-combining (MemType::WC: x86 PAT index 1, aarch64 Normal
      non-cacheable, riscv64 Svpbmt NC when RV_SVPBMT finds the extension,
      else the plain PTE) and the boot prints one [FB] line. n: no
      framebuffer code is compiled.

config FB_CONSOLE
    # cargo-feature: fb-console
    bool "Framebuffer text console (mirrors the UART console)"
    default n
    depends on FB_GENERIC
    help
      Kernel feature `fb-console` (crates/drivers/fb/src/console.rs): an
      8x16 text console that mirrors every byte the UART console writes,
      with scrolling and a dirty-rect flush. The grid is
      FB_CONSOLE_COLS_MAX x FB_CONSOLE_ROWS_MAX bytes of kernel memory. A
      line that finds the grid busy is not drawn (the UART still has it).
      No keyboard focus and no cursor of its own. The grid also publishes
      what is on the screen, FB_CONSOLE_SCROLLBACK lines of scrollback and the
      mouse reporting modes programs set into a region the ring-3 terminal
      server maps (MemObj console.screen), and draws the overlay that server
      asks for: a pointer cell and a selection in inverse video, and the view
      scrolled back.

config FB_PANIC_SCREEN
    # cargo-feature: fb-panic-screen
    bool "Framebuffer panic screen"
    default n
    depends on FB_GENERIC
    help
      Kernel feature `fb-panic-screen` (crates/drivers/fb/src/panic.rs):
      the panic report (banner, [PANIC] policy and reason, location,
      message, hart and task) drawn straight onto the write-combining
      mapping, lock-free, so a panic inside the console or with the heap
      gone still reaches the screen. The text is the same the UART gets.

config INPUTD
    bool "Input server (INPUTD.ELF): the focus is a warrant, the Secure Attention Key is a trusted path"
    depends on DRV_VIRTIO_INPUT || DRV_PS2
    depends on CAP_SLOT_EXT
    default n
    help
      The built-in topology carries a row for INPUTD.ELF and the kernel starts
      it at boot (userspace/services/inputd, the Input interface of
      crates/core/devif/src/input.rs). The input drivers (virtio-input, PS/2,
      USB HID) then also write a stream of every key, button and axis event
      into a second ring of the event region (crates/drivers/input/src/
      events.rs), keys that make console bytes included. The server reads the
      stream and delivers it only to the task that holds the focus: a task
      asks for it with `take_focus` and is lent a capability on the server's
      endpoint (the warrant) and a ring of its own events. Moving the focus
      revokes the old warrant and closes the old ring. Ctrl+Alt+Delete is
      never delivered: the server takes the focus from whoever has it and
      counts the chord on a page that tasks map read-only, for a compositor
      to read.

      The server is the one arbiter of the keyboard and the pointer: while
      nobody holds the focus they are the console's (typed bytes go to the
      terminal server, which drains the first ring: arrows, F-keys, the mouse
      on the framebuffer console), so a build with no graphical client sees no
      change on the shell; while a task holds it, the drivers give the console
      nothing of it but the modifier keys and the chord (the region's focus
      word, which the server writes). A serial line is the console's always.
      The drivers wake the server when they push into an empty stream (a
      futex on the region), so no timer is involved. The toolbox applet
      `input watch` is a client. Needs CAP_SLOT_EXT: a warrant is a
      capability with a badge only the server names. Cost when set: one task
      of 64 pages (INPUTD_MEM_PAGES in the topology builder), a memory object
      per focus grant and one for the trusted page, and one more ring push and
      a read of the focus word per event in the drivers' interrupt arms. n
      keeps the stream out of the drivers and the console owns the keyboard.

config COMPOSITOR
    bool "Compositor (COMPSRV.ELF): the sole holder of the Display interface, the trusted path's banner"
    depends on INPUTD && FB_GENERIC
    default n
    help
      The built-in topology carries a row for COMPSRV.ELF and the kernel
      starts it at boot (userspace/services/compositor, the Compositor
      interface of userspace/lib/compositor). It claims the display
      connector (crates/core/devif/src/display.rs: the kernel's text
      console stops drawing), keeps one full-screen surface made of
      physically contiguous bands, and composes its clients' windows into it
      with userspace/lib/compose, presenting only the damage the clients
      post. A client makes a memory object, draws in it and attaches it with
      `attach`; tearing is tolerated (a present copies while the client may
      still be drawing). No other row holds the Display interface.

      It is also the trusted surface of the Secure Attention Key
      (Ctrl+Alt+Delete, INPUTD): its row alone holds a call right on
      endpoint.input whose badge the kernel stamps (EXEC beside WRITE,
      azos_devif::input::TRUSTED_BADGE). After the chord the input server
      refuses every other caller the focus (DENIED, recorded) until this
      server takes it, which it does after it has drawn the trusted-path
      banner, COMPOSITOR_BANNER_MS later; the banner and the "why was this
      refused" panel (the newest refusals the decision server holds) are
      drawn by this server alone. Without this option the chord only revokes
      and any holder of the call right takes the focus straight back.

      Needs a framebuffer (FB_GENERIC: virtio-gpu, ramfb, Limine, device
      tree) and the input server. A build with no display behind the
      connector still runs the server: it acknowledges the chord without a
      banner, so the focus stays obtainable. Memory objects (MAX_MEMOBJS):
      one per 64 pages of the screen (a 640x480 XRGB8888 screen is 5, a
      1280x800 one 16, and a task may own half of the table) plus one per
      attached window of at most 64 pages. Cost when set: one task whose
      frame ceiling is COMPOSITOR_SURFACE_PAGES plus the 192 the server
      itself needs (crates/core/topology/src/builder.rs, COMPOSITOR_MEM_PAGES).

config COMPOSITOR_BANNER_MS
    int "Compositor: milliseconds the trusted-path banner holds the focus gate after the Secure Attention Key"
    range 100 10000
    default 1500
    help
      Only with COMPOSITOR. userspace/services/compositor. After Ctrl+Alt+Delete the input server
      refuses the focus to every caller but the compositor. The compositor
      draws the banner at once and takes the focus (opening the gate for the
      plain callers) this long afterwards. It is the time the user has to
      see that the chord was seen by the system and not by the application
      that was in front, before any application can ask for the keyboard
      again. Too short and the banner may go unseen; too long and the
      keyboard is out of reach of every application for that long after each
      chord.

config COMPOSITOR_FRAME_MS
    int "Compositor: milliseconds between two looks at the clients' damage and the trusted page"
    range 4 100
    default 16
    help
      Only with COMPOSITOR. userspace/services/compositor. A client's damage is posted into a
      table, not presented in the call; the compositor thread composes and
      presents what accumulated, and reads the Secure Attention Key's count on
      the trusted-path page, every this many milliseconds. It is the latency
      a damage rectangle adds before it is on the screen, and the time the
      banner can lag the chord. Lower is more wake-ups on an idle screen.

config COMPOSITOR_SURFACE_PAGES
    int "Compositor: 4 KiB pages of the screen's surface the server's row may hold"
    range 64 2048
    default 320
    help
      Only with COMPOSITOR. userspace/services/compositor. The full-screen
      surface is cut into physically contiguous bands of at most 64 pages,
      each a memory object the compositor creates, so their frames are
      charged to its Domain like any other memory it holds
      (crates/core/topology/src/memory.rs: a row's ceiling covers the shared
      memory it created). Its row's ceiling is this plus what the server
      itself needs. A 640x480 XRGB8888 screen takes 5 bands of 64 pages
      (320, the default: QEMU's ramfb); 1280x800 takes 16 (1024). A screen
      whose bands need more is not claimed: the server says so and runs
      headless. Cost: the ceiling is counted by the boot's memory admission
      (the ring-3 rows' ceilings against the free pages); the frames are
      taken only for the screen there is.
